## What {{.Name}} is

Four surfaces, one binary:

- **CLI** — interactive + scriptable. Stable schemas, structured errors,
  preview via `--dry-run`, queries parse via `--explain`.
- **Capability surface** — `{{.Name}} spec --format json` emits a
  machine-readable manifest of every command, its inputs, outputs, side
  effects, and idempotency. Agents drive {{.Name}} from this, not from
  `--help`.
- **Served commands** — `{{.Name}} serve` projects the same commands
  onto REST, a local Unix socket, MCP, and gRPC, behind the same
  safety policy. See [Serve it](#serve-it).
- **Library** — `import "{{.Module}}"` and use the same primitives from
  your own Go code.

Conformance proof: [`docs/12-factor-conformance.md`](docs/12-factor-conformance.md)
(auto-regenerated by `make conformance`).

## Serve it

Every command in `cmd/` is also a route. There is nothing to mount:

```sh
{{.Name}} serve --list        # the registered services and their state
{{.Name}} serve api           # REST on 127.0.0.1:8080
{{.Name}} serve socket        # the same commands over an owner-only Unix socket
{{.Name}} serve mcp           # the same commands as MCP tools on 127.0.0.1:8081/mcp
{{.Name}} serve mcp --stdio   # MCP over stdin/stdout, for a host that spawns {{.Name}}
{{.Name}} serve rpc           # the same commands to gRPC, Connect and gRPC-Web clients on 127.0.0.1:8082
{{.Name}} serve               # every enabled service under one lifecycle
```

`serve --list` names every registered service and, per service,
whether the supervisor has a configuration for it (CONFIGURED — from
your config file, or from `api`'s built-in default), whether a bare
`serve` would start it (ENABLED — `api` is on by default, `socket`,
`mcp` and `rpc` are off until you enable them), and whether it is accepting work
right now (READY — true only inside a running `serve`, so always false
when you ask from the shell). A fresh project prints:

```console
$ {{.Name}} serve --list
SERVICE              CONFIGURED  ENABLED  READY
api                  true        true     false
socket               false       false    false
mcp                  false       false    false
rpc                  false       false    false
```

```sh
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/commands | jq '.commands[] | {name, invocable, reason}'
curl -s 'http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1/commands/hello?arg=Ada'
```

```json
{"exit_code":0,"data":[{"name":"Ada","message":"Hello, Ada!"}]}
```

What holds on every served surface:

- **Loopback by default.** The api listens on `127.0.0.1:8080`, the
  mcp service on `127.0.0.1:8081` and the rpc service on
  `127.0.0.1:8082`; each refuses, at exit 2, to serve unauthenticated
  anywhere else. Set `Auth` in `cmd/root.go` to serve beyond loopback,
  or opt in by name with `services.api.insecure_remote: true`
  (`services.mcp.insecure_remote` for MCP, `services.rpc.insecure_remote`
  for gRPC).
- **Reads are `GET`, writes are `POST`, destructive commands are
  withheld** until you name the surface in `APIConfig.Policy`,
  `SocketConfig.Policy`, `mcpserve.Config.Policy` or
  `rpcserve.Config.Policy` — and even then the command's own `confirm`
  gate still applies. Over MCP a withheld command is simply not in the
  tool list; over gRPC it is refused with `permission_denied`.
- **Discovery tells you why.** A command that is not mounted still
  appears in `/v1/commands` with `invocable: false` and a reason:
  `unauthorized-destructive`, `interactive`, `self-hosting`,
  `management-only`.
- **Readiness is in the trace.** The `ready_reported` log line carries
  the bound address under `address=`; wait for it rather than for a
  banner.

Move the listen address into configuration rather than a flag:

```yaml
# ~/.config/{{.Name}}/config.yaml
services:
  api:
    addr: 127.0.0.1:9090
  socket:
    enabled: true
  mcp:
    addr: 127.0.0.1:9091
  rpc:
    addr: 127.0.0.1:9092
```

Add a service of your own with `cli.WithService` in `cmd/root.go`; it
appears in `serve --list` beside `api`, `socket`, `mcp` and `rpc` and
starts under the same supervisor.

[`AGENTS.md`](AGENTS.md) is the agent-facing description of this
surface: discovery, the refusal vocabulary, invocation over REST, the
socket, MCP and gRPC, the confirmation gates, and the exit-code taxonomy.
Hand it to an agent that has to call `{{.Name}}` without a terminal.

### Connect an MCP host

Claude Desktop, Claude Code, Cursor and other hosts that read an
`mcpServers` map spawn `{{.Name}}` and speak MCP over its stdin and
stdout. Add this entry to the host's configuration —
`claude_desktop_config.json` for Claude Desktop, `.mcp.json` at the
project root for Claude Code, `mcp.json` for Cursor:

```json
{
  "mcpServers": {
    "{{.Name}}": {
      "command": "{{.Name}}",
      "args": ["serve", "mcp", "--stdio"]
    }
  }
}
```

A desktop app does not read your shell's `PATH`, so give `command` the
absolute path (`which {{.Name}}`) when the host cannot find the binary.
Every command that may run is one tool, named by its path with dots
(`item list` is `item.list`); its flags, and its positional arguments
when it declares them in `kit/args`, are the tool's arguments.

What the host can call:

- **Commands marked `kit/auth-required` run over stdio** on the trust
  of the process that spawned `{{.Name}}`: it already runs as you. Over
  HTTP they are refused until you set `mcpserve.Config.Auth`.
- **Commands marked `kit/requires-confirmation` ask the host's user**
  to approve each call. A host that cannot ask gets `confirmation
  required`; over HTTP the `X-Confirm-Token` header answers instead.
- **Destructive commands stay off the tool list** until
  `mcpserve.Config.Policy` names `cmdsurface.SurfaceMCP`.

Serving MCP links the MCP Go SDK into `{{.Name}}`: about 2 MB on a
stripped release build, 3 MB with symbols. If you will never serve
MCP, delete the `mcpserve.With(...)` option and its import from
`cmd/root.go` and the SDK drops out of the build.

### Call it over gRPC

`serve rpc` serves every command as one typed service,
`cmdsurface.v1.Commands`, defined in
[`commands.proto`](https://github.com/hop-top/poly-kit/blob/main/contracts/proto/cmdsurface/v1/commands.proto).
A call names the command by path, with its flags and positional
arguments; adding a command to `cmd/` needs no new stubs. One port
answers Connect (JSON and binary protobuf), gRPC-Web, and gRPC over
unencrypted HTTP/2. Connect JSON is plain HTTP, so `curl` works:

```sh
{{.Name}} serve rpc
curl -s http://127.0.0.1:8082/cmdsurface.v1.Commands/Invoke \
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \
  -d '{"path":["hello"],"args":["Ada"]}'
```

```json
{"exit_code":0,"data":[{"message":"Hello, Ada!","name":"Ada"}],"data_json":"[{\"message\":\"Hello, Ada!\",\"name\":\"Ada\"}]"}
```

Go clients use the stubs kit ships in
`hop.top/kit/go/transport/cmdsurface/gen/cmdsurfacev1`; other languages
generate theirs from the proto file. A command that ran and failed is
still a successful call, so read `exit_code`; a refusal is an RPC
error with a code, and nothing ran. `InvokeStream` returns each output
line as it is written, then the result.

Serving gRPC adds about 0.4 MB to a stripped release build of
`{{.Name}}`, 0.6 MB with symbols: kit already links the Connect and
protobuf runtimes it uses. If you will never serve gRPC, delete the
`rpcserve.With(...)` option and its import from `cmd/root.go`.

## Configuration

Config file: `~/.config/{{.Name}}/config.yaml` (also
`/etc/{{.Name}}/config.yaml` and the nearest `.kit/config.yaml`,
`.kit.yaml`, or `kit.yaml` walking up from the working directory).
Environment variables prefixed with `{{.NameUpper}}_` override the
files: `{{.NameUpper}}_SERVICES_API_ADDR` sets `services.api.addr`.
`-c <path>` merges one more file; `-c key=value` overrides one key.

